  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA continental polar airmass along with a strong surface high is\nin place today into Friday with cool 850 mb temperatures and \ndry Northeast flow aloft. Conditions the next few nights are \nfavorable for radiational cooling so depending on how much the \nboundary layer dries out today and tomorrow afternoon of how \nmuch high clouds overspread the region from the developing \ncoastal low will dictate frost potential. Northern Oneida has \nthe best chance at frost as dry air is already in place around \nthe Tug Hill and it will be hard for any high clouds to get that\nfar north. For CNY into the Twin Tiers of NY and PA, dew points\nwill have to mix down to the high 30s and skies remain clear \nall night to give a chance at some patchy frost in deeper, \nelevated valleys. If the dew points remain in the 40s, then fog \ndevelopment would be more likely preventing a frost. Given that \nthe frost in Northern Oneida looks spotty at best as \ntemperatures likely remain at or just above freezing in coldest \nlocations, no frost headline has been put out yet at this time. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nHeading into this weekend, a strong early season noreaster \nforms off the east coast of the US and approaches the Northeast.\nThere is still uncertainty in its exact track as there is not a\nwhole lot of strong steering yet with a relatively weak jet \nstream. With the strong surface high in place currently, it will\nbe tough for the storm to work its way into it much so \nconfidence is at least greater for the bulk of the precipitation\nand wind to remain east. Ensemble low pressure low tracks still\nbring quite a few members (~20%) well west into PA and NJ. \nThose tracks would bring heavier rain and wind through the \nweekend but those remain outliers at this time. \n\nClouds will still wrap in behind the low into our area so we \nwill at least see cloudy skies and an increase in the winds. The\nCatskill could get some rain with sprinkles and occasional \nlight rain farther west. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \n\nOnce the noreaster weakens and moves out to sea early next \nweek, upper level ridging builds back in with SW flow aloft. \nThis will help lead to a gradual warming period with \ntemperatures returning to normal to slightly above normal. \nChances of rain look low early next week as well given dry air \nadvecting in from the central US under the building ridge.",
